==================================================
Lab 6: File Path Traversal, Validation of File Extension with Null Byte Bypass
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website loads product images through a URL that includes the filename.
The server checks that the filename ends with an image extension, such as .png, before it reads the file.
A null byte placed before the extension makes the file system stop reading the name early, so the check passes but the wrong file is opened.

Goal: read the contents of /etc/passwd.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

When a product image loads, the browser requests /image?filename=<name>.png.
The server confirms the name ends in .png, then reads the file from /var/www/images/ and returns it.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The extension check looks at the whole string, including the .png at the end.
The file system call that opens the file is written in C, and C treats a null byte as the end of a string.
So the check and the file open read the same value differently.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Null byte (%00): A character with the value zero. Encoded in a URL, it appears as %00.
Extension check: Confirms the name ends in an allowed suffix, such as .png.
Early termination: When the file system reaches a null byte, it stops reading the name there, so anything after it is ignored.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

Instead of a plain filename, we send:

../../../etc/passwd%00.png

The server's check sees a name ending in .png, so it allows the request.
When the file is opened, the null byte ends the name at ../../../etc/passwd, and the .png is ignored.
The three ../ move up to /, and the server reads /etc/passwd.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Open the lab and load any product image.
Replace the filename in the image URL with ../../../etc/passwd%00.png.
Load the page. The text of /etc/passwd appears, and the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It asks for the lab's base URL and builds the full URL with %00.png already in it.
* It sends a GET request to that URL. The payload is not passed through params, so %00 is not encoded a second time.
* If the server returns status 200, it prints the response text, which is the contents of /etc/passwd.
* If the request fails or the status is different, it prints an error instead.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The extension check and the file open treat the same string differently.
The check reads the whole value, while the file system stops at the null byte, so the file it opens is not the one the check approved.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not build file paths from user input. Map each image to an ID or a fixed name on the server.
Reject any input that contains a null byte before doing any other check.
Resolve the final path and confirm it is inside the image folder, using the same value that will be opened.

Other good practices:

Run the web server with only the file permissions it needs.
Keep sensitive files outside the paths the application can reach.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
A check and a file open can disagree about where a string ends when a null byte is present.
Validate the exact value that will be used to open the file.
Never trust a filename that comes from the user.
